"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64","sessionId":"c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"claude/orca-import-menu","slug":"dapper-knitting-moler"}
{"parentUuid":"067f9549-290c-4d38-ae63-98ad5258f67f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4fcc31c2-3e80-420b-a5c6-f7cbf63689ec","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AtFSd5PGHc5MpdQUeRSvuH","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nname: live-test-built-app-on-mac\ndescription: \"How to launch, see, and drive the locally built JusPrin app on the Mac: the shared bundle id binds tools to the wrong instance, and \\\"on another Space\\\" usually means the window is on the second monitor, not that the user is in full screen.\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: fa2d66ed-e217-440b-aae0-da4164953edc\n  modified: 2026-11-01T19:29:08.107Z\n---\n\nEvery build of the fork (and /Applications/OrcaSlicer.app) shares bundle id `com.softfever3d.orca-slicer`. The desktop-control tools resolve an app by bundle id and bind to whichever instance comes first, so `app_list_windows` returns `[]` and filtered screenshots hide the window you actually launched.\n\n**Working recipe (2026-09-05):** `ditto` the built .app into the scratchpad, `plutil -replace CFBundleIdentifier -string com.jusprin.worktree-test`, `codesign --force --deep -s -`, then `open -n <copy>.app --args <model>` and `request_access` for the new id. `app_screenshot`/`app_click` then work and include popup menus. A display-scope `left_click` on the header did NOT open the fork's PopupWindow menus, but a background `app_click` did; take the pixel-level `zoom` with the display tools afterwards (call `app_release` first). Display-scope `Escape` does not close those popups; a click elsewhere does.\n\nThe MCP discovery file `~/Library/Application Support/JusPrin2/jusprin/mcp.json` points at the newest instance's `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/mcp`; `tests/mcp/probe.py`'s `call()` can drive real tools (e.g. `workspace_inspect`, then `report_slice_review` with sessionId/plateId/sliceResultId) to put the header into the Check-print state without lldb. lldb `expr` cannot call inline accessors like `status_row()`.\n\n**Why:** On 2026-09-01 a verification pass screenshotted the stock app instead of the build; on 2026-09-05 the tools bound to another session's main-repo build.\n\n**How to apply:** Confirm the pid with `pgrep -fl OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS`; never kill an instance you did not launch. `osascript` System Events by unix id still works to set `frontmost`. See [[jusprin-shell-phase-1-design]] and [[orca-inherited-teardown-crash]].\n\n**\"On another Space\" usually means \"on the other monitor.\"** Cost about ten turns on\n2026-09-09 and produced a wrong report to Kenneth. `app_list_windows` returns\n`is_off_space: true` and clicks are refused with \"this window is on another Space\";\n`app_bring_to_current_space` then fails with \"the user's current Space is a full-screen\napp.\" Both messages are about Spaces, so the natural read is that the user is sitting in\na full-screen app — but with *Displays have separate Spaces* on, a window merely sitting\non the second monitor produces exactly the same symptoms. Kenneth was not in full screen;\nthe harness had opened on the Acer.\n\nTell them apart before saying anything to the user: take a **display-scope** screenshot\n(`app_release`, then `computer_batch` screenshot). Its header names the monitor it\ncaptured and lists the others — if the window shows up there, it is a monitor problem,\nnot a Space problem, and the display-scope tools can drive it immediately. Never conclude\n\"the user is in a full-screen app\" from the background tools' error text alone.\n\nThe same flag also flaps: a window can read `is_off_space: false` and click fine, then\nread `true` a minute later. Clicks delivered while it is true report `ok ... unverified`\nand silently do nothing. **A click that reports \"unverified\" and changes nothing is not\nevidence of an application bug** — on 2026-09-09 that nearly became a filed defect about\nbeing unable to retry after a failed MCP connect. Re-verify on a reachable window before\nconcluding anything about the app.\n\n**Prefer the shell harness over a re-signed app copy for Agent-panel work.**\n`tests/shell/.../JusPrinShellHarness.app` already carries its own bundle id\n(`io.jusprin.shell-harness`), so none of the bundle-id collision above applies, and\n`--manual-unconfigured` lands directly on the dock's not-set-up state — one click from\nthe Agent setup chooser. Build target is `shell_integration_harness`, not `shell_harness`.\n\n**Isolate `$HOME` when testing MCP client setup.** `Mcp::default_catalog_paths()` reads\n`$HOME`, so the flow will happily rewrite the real `~/.cursor/mcp.json`. Launch with\n`HOME=<scratch>` holding a `.cursor/mcp.json` fixture; detection, the diff, and the write\nall follow it. Force a write failure with `chmod 444` on the file **and** `555` on its\ndirectory — the directory alone is not enough.\n\n**Re-signed copies trigger system prompts.** Each rebuild + ad-hoc re-sign changes the cdhash, so macOS re-asks for Downloads access (recent-project thumbnail load blocks in `fopen`) and Keychain access (agent runtime reads the API key). Both are prompts only the user can answer. Avoid them by running the binary directly (`nohup <copy>.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir <scratch copy of JusPrin2 with recent_projects cleared> <model>`) with `OPENAI_API_KEY=<placeholder>` in the environment, which short-circuits the Keychain lookup in `AgentConfiguration.cpp`. Use `sample <pid>` on the main thread to see which prompt is blocking.\n\n**Stale as of 2026-09-18 (branch claude/printer-conversation-panel-af11cb):** `load_agent_runtime` reads the key only from app config (`jusprin_agent` / `openai_api_key`); no env var or Keychain lookup exists there. In the shell harness, `OPENAI_API_KEY` only feeds the docked pane via `install_harness_agent`; the printer panel builds its own runtime from app config, so `--printer-live` writes the key into the run's throwaway conf. Pass it as `std::string`: `AppConfig::set(section, key, const char*)` silently picks the `bool` overload and stores `true` (seen as a 401 \"key rejected\"). The harness temp data dir then holds the real key: delete `/var/folders/*/*/T/jusprin-shell-*` dirs from those runs afterwards.\n\n**Hands-on printer panel with a live model (2026-09-23, jusprin-newui, uncommitted at the time):** `--manual-live-agent` now also writes `OPENAI_API_KEY` into the run's app config, so Home → \"+ Add printer\" gets a live printer chat. Feed the key from `~/Library/Application Support/JusPrin2/JusPrin2.conf` (`jusprin_agent.openai_api_key`) via env without echoing it. Drive it with display-scope `computer_batch` clicks/typing (background `app_click` does nothing on the web view). The scripted `--printer-live` run hangs when the model doesn't pick the printer its next \"tap\" step expects; kill it. Afterwards `rm -rf` the run's `/var/folders/*/*/T/jusprin-shell-*` dir — killed runs leave it behind with the key.\n\n---\nname: open-file-in-app-without-screen-control\ndescription: \"Drive \\\"open a file\\\" in a scratch copy of the built app with no screen control — AppleScript open addressed to the copy's path works; hidden Home page timers don't run; always override the model endpoint first.\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: reference\n  originSessionId: 72fc1a8c-e5fe-483e-9283-9be1cc811037\n  modified: 2026-10-28T15:37:18.379Z\n---\n\n2026-10-28 (phantom edit rows, critique 1B-02): Kenneth declined computer-use control of the app. What worked for \"open a file in the running app\" (the Finder path → `GUI_App::MacOpenFiles` → `ShellController::take_opened_files`):\n\n`osascript -e 'tell application \"<scratch>/App.app\" to open POSIX file \"<file>\"'` — addresses the copy by its bundle path, so it reaches that instance and not another session's `io.jusprin.JusPrin`. It prints \"missing value\" and works. `open -g -a <path> <file>` was unreliable (lsappinfo held stale dead entries for the same path); if you use it, add `--args --datadir <scratch>` so a fresh launch can't touch the real data folder.\n\nDo NOT Finder-open a `.3mf` this way: with `single_instance` off, `MacOpenFiles` calls `start_new_slicer`, a second instance on the default (real) data dir.\n\nThe Home page's injected-script approach ([[live-host-check-via-instrumented-dev-page]]) fails for Home actions while Prepare is showing: the hidden Home web view runs no timers. The Agent page snapshot (`snap` → outerHTML → headless Chrome) still works for screenshots.\n\nOpening a model starts an automatic first agent turn with the saved key. Set `JUSPRIN_AGENT_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9/v1` before launching, or a live model call goes out (it did once that day). See [[prompt-eval-spend-budget]].\n\nFor which code path published something, `lldb -b -s cmds` with `breakpoint set -n \"<fully qualified name>\"` + `bt 60` + `continue` works; `-r \"...$\"` regexes resolved to nothing.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f9b8db2c-e18f-4bcb-bdc6-8a8b87ddda73","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:06:52.770Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\nname: live-test-built-app-on-mac\ndescription: \"How to launch, see, and drive the locally built JusPrin app on the Mac: the shared bundle id binds tools to the wrong instance, and \\\"on another Space\\\" usually means the window is on the second monitor, not that the user is in full screen.\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: fa2d66ed-e217-440b-aae0-da4164953edc\n  modified: 2026-11-01T19:29:08.107Z\n---\n\nEvery build of the fork (and /Applications/OrcaSlicer.app) shares bundle id `com.softfever3d.orca-slicer`. The desktop-control tools resolve an app by bundle id and bind to whichever instance comes first, so `app_list_windows` returns `[]` and filtered screenshots hide the window you actually launched.\n\n**Working recipe (2026-09-05):** `ditto` the built .app into the scratchpad, `plutil -replace CFBundleIdentifier -string com.jusprin.worktree-test`, `codesign --force --deep -s -`, then `open -n <copy>.app --args <model>` and `request_access` for the new id. `app_screenshot`/`app_click` then work and include popup menus. A display-scope `left_click` on the header did NOT open the fork's PopupWindow menus, but a background `app_click` did; take the pixel-level `zoom` with the display tools afterwards (call `app_release` first). Display-scope `Escape` does not close those popups; a click elsewhere does.\n\nThe MCP discovery file `~/Library/Application Support/JusPrin2/jusprin/mcp.json` points at the newest instance's `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/mcp`; `tests/mcp/probe.py`'s `call()` can drive real tools (e.g. `workspace_inspect`, then `report_slice_review` with sessionId/plateId/sliceResultId) to put the header into the Check-print state without lldb. lldb `expr` cannot call inline accessors like `status_row()`.\n\n**Why:** On 2026-09-01 a verification pass screenshotted the stock app instead of the build; on 2026-09-05 the tools bound to another session's main-repo build.\n\n**How to apply:** Confirm the pid with `pgrep -fl OrcaSlicer.app/Contents/MacOS`; never kill an instance you did not launch. `osascript` System Events by unix id still works to set `frontmost`. See [[jusprin-shell-phase-1-design]] and [[orca-inherited-teardown-crash]].\n\n**\"On another Space\" usually means \"on the other monitor.\"** Cost about ten turns on\n2026-09-09 and produced a wrong report to Kenneth. `app_list_windows` returns\n`is_off_space: true` and clicks are refused with \"this window is on another Space\";\n`app_bring_to_current_space` then fails with \"the user's current Space is a full-screen\napp.\" Both messages are about Spaces, so the natural read is that the user is sitting in\na full-screen app — but with *Displays have separate Spaces* on, a window merely sitting\non the second monitor produces exactly the same symptoms. Kenneth was not in full screen;\nthe harness had opened on the Acer.\n\nTell them apart before saying anything to the user: take a **display-scope** screenshot\n(`app_release`, then `computer_batch` screenshot). Its header names the monitor it\ncaptured and lists the others — if the window shows up there, it is a monitor problem,\nnot a Space problem, and the display-scope tools can drive it immediately. Never conclude\n\"the user is in a full-screen app\" from the background tools' error text alone.\n\nThe same flag also flaps: a window can read `is_off_space: false` and click fine, then\nread `true` a minute later. Clicks delivered while it is true report `ok ... unverified`\nand silently do nothing. **A click that reports \"unverified\" and changes nothing is not\nevidence of an application bug** — on 2026-09-09 that nearly became a filed defect about\nbeing unable to retry after a failed MCP connect. Re-verify on a reachable window before\nconcluding anything about the app.\n\n**Prefer the shell harness over a re-signed app copy for Agent-panel work.**\n`tests/shell/.../JusPrinShellHarness.app` already carries its own bundle id\n(`io.jusprin.shell-harness`), so none of the bundle-id collision above applies, and\n`--manual-unconfigured` lands directly on the dock's not-set-up state — one click from\nthe Agent setup chooser. Build target is `shell_integration_harness`, not `shell_harness`.\n\n**Isolate `$HOME` when testing MCP client setup.** `Mcp::default_catalog_paths()` reads\n`$HOME`, so the flow will happily rewrite the real `~/.cursor/mcp.json`. Launch with\n`HOME=<scratch>` holding a `.cursor/mcp.json` fixture; detection, the diff, and the write\nall follow it. Force a write failure with `chmod 444` on the file **and** `555` on its\ndirectory — the directory alone is not enough.\n\n**Re-signed copies trigger system prompts.** Each rebuild + ad-hoc re-sign changes the cdhash, so macOS re-asks for Downloads access (recent-project thumbnail load blocks in `fopen`) and Keychain access (agent runtime reads the API key). Both are prompts only the user can answer. Avoid them by running the binary directly (`nohup <copy>.app/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir <scratch copy of JusPrin2 with recent_projects cleared> <model>`) with `OPENAI_API_KEY=<placeholder>` in the environment, which short-circuits the Keychain lookup in `AgentConfiguration.cpp`. Use `sample <pid>` on the main thread to see which prompt is blocking.\n\n**Stale as of 2026-09-18 (branch claude/printer-conversation-panel-af11cb):** `load_agent_runtime` reads the key only from app config (`jusprin_agent` / `openai_api_key`); no env var or Keychain lookup exists there. In the shell harness, `OPENAI_API_KEY` only feeds the docked pane via `install_harness_agent`; the printer panel builds its own runtime from app config, so `--printer-live` writes the key into the run's throwaway conf. Pass it as `std::string`: `AppConfig::set(section, key, const char*)` silently picks the `bool` overload and stores `true` (seen as a 401 \"key rejected\"). The harness temp data dir then holds the real key: delete `/var/folders/*/*/T/jusprin-shell-*` dirs from those runs afterwards.\n\n**Hands-on printer panel with a live model (2026-09-23, jusprin-newui, uncommitted at the time):** `--manual-live-agent` now also writes `OPENAI_API_KEY` into the run's app config, so Home → \"+ Add printer\" gets a live printer chat. Feed the key from `~/Library/Application Support/JusPrin2/JusPrin2.conf` (`jusprin_agent.openai_api_key`) via env without echoing it. Drive it with display-scope `computer_batch` clicks/typing (background `app_click` does nothing on the web view). The scripted `--printer-live` run hangs when the model doesn't pick the printer its next \"tap\" step expects; kill it. Afterwards `rm -rf` the run's `/var/folders/*/*/T/jusprin-shell-*` dir — killed runs leave it behind with the key.\n\n---\nname: open-file-in-app-without-screen-control\ndescription: \"Drive \\\"open a file\\\" in a scratch copy of the built app with no screen control — AppleScript open addressed to the copy's path works; hidden Home page timers don't run; always override the model endpoint first.\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: reference\n  originSessionId: 72fc1a8c-e5fe-483e-9283-9be1cc811037\n  modified: 2026-10-28T15:37:18.379Z\n---\n\n2026-10-28 (phantom edit rows, critique 1B-02): Kenneth declined computer-use control of the app. What worked for \"open a file in the running app\" (the Finder path → `GUI_App::MacOpenFiles` → `ShellController::take_opened_files`):\n\n`osascript -e 'tell application \"<scratch>/App.app\" to open POSIX file \"<file>\"'` — addresses the copy by its bundle path, so it reaches that instance and not another session's `io.jusprin.JusPrin`. It prints \"missing value\" and works. `open -g -a <path> <file>` was unreliable (lsappinfo held stale dead entries for the same path); if you use it, add `--args --datadir <scratch>` so a fresh launch can't touch the real data folder.\n\nDo NOT Finder-open a `.3mf` this way: with `single_instance` off, `MacOpenFiles` calls `start_new_slicer`, a second instance on the default (real) data dir.\n\nThe Home page's injected-script approach ([[live-host-check-via-instrumented-dev-page]]) fails for Home actions while Prepare is showing: the hidden Home web view runs no timers. The Agent page snapshot (`snap` → outerHTML → headless Chrome) still works for screenshots.\n\nOpening a model starts an automatic first agent turn with the saved key. Set `JUSPRIN_AGENT_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9/v1` before launching, or a live model call goes out (it did once that day). See [[prompt-eval-spend-budget]].\n\nFor which code path published something, `lldb -b -s cmds` with `breakpoint set -n \"<fully qualified name>\"` + `bt 60` + `continue` works; `-r \"...$\"` regexes resolved to nothing.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"067f9549-290c-4d38-ae63-98ad5258f67f","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/build/arm64","sessionId":"c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"claude/orca-import-menu","slug":"dapper-knitting-moler"}
